10. CRUD Explanation
Create — StudentController.createStudent validates the request body (@Valid), and StudentServiceImpl.createStudent checks for duplicate register numbers/emails before saving via StudentRepository.save.
Read — getAllStudents returns the full register; getStudentById throws ResourceNotFoundException (→ 404) if the ID doesn't exist.
Update — updateStudent loads the existing record, re-validates uniqueness against other students (so a student can keep their own register number/email), then applies field-by-field changes and saves.
Delete — deleteStudent checks existence first so a clean 404 is returned instead of a silent no-op.
11. Validation
Frontend (js/app.js, validateForm): checks required fields, email format, phone format, and CGPA range before any network call, and displays inline error messages under each field.
Backend (Jakarta Bean Validation on the Student entity + GlobalExceptionHandler): re-validates everything server-side regardless of what the client sent, since frontend validation alone can always be bypassed.
Rule
Enforced
Name required
Frontend + backend (@NotBlank)
Register number required
Frontend + backend (@NotBlank)
Department required
Frontend + backend (@NotBlank)
Valid email format
Frontend + backend (@Email)
CGPA between 0–10
Frontend + backend (@DecimalMin/@DecimalMax)
Duplicate register number rejected
Backend service check + DB unique constraint
Duplicate email rejected
Backend service check + DB unique constraint
12. Testing
Postman
The full collection is in postman/Placement_Management_API.postman_collection.json. Import it into Postman, set the baseUrl variable (default http://localhost:8080), and run it as a collection. It covers:
Create with valid data
Create with missing fields
Create with invalid email
Create with duplicate register number
Get all students
Get student by ID
Get invalid ID
Update student
Update invalid ID
Delete student
Delete invalid ID
Search students
Dashboard statistics
Automated tests
mvn test runs a Spring context-load smoke test against an in-memory H2 database, so tests never touch your real MySQL data.
Frontend
Verified manually in Chrome DevTools device toolbar at both a desktop width (1440px) and a mobile width (375px). Below 860px the sidebar collapses into a horizontal top navigation bar and the two-column form becomes a single column.
13. Installation & Running Locally
Prerequisites
JDK 17+
Maven 3.8+
MySQL 8+ running locally (or accessible remotely)
Steps
Bash
The application starts on http://localhost:8080.
